<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Castillon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1453">1453</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Castillon was fought on 17 July 1453 near Castillon on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, and is regarded as the last battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. An English army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-talbot-1st-earl-of-shrewsbury">John Talbot</a>, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, attacked a fortified French camp and was destroyed by French artillery, and Talbot was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> surrendered in October, ending three centuries of English rule in Gascony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-castillon.jpg" alt="Battle of Castillon" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fran%C3%A7ais_5054,_fol._229v,_Bataille_de_Castillon_1453_-_d%C3%A9tail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the French took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> in 1451 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> seemed over, but its citizens, after three hundred years of Plantagenet rule, saw themselves as subjects of the English king and asked Henry VI to recapture the province. On 17 October 1452 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-talbot-1st-earl-of-shrewsbury">John Talbot</a>, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, landed near Bordeaux with 3,000 men. He was rumoured to be seventy-five or eighty but was more likely about sixty-six. With the townspeople's cooperation he took the city on 23 October, and by the end of the year the English held most of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. The French had expected an expedition through Normandy and spent the winter preparing a counterattack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Charles VII invaded Guyenne with three armies, and the French besieged Castillon on 8 July. Yielding to the town leaders' pleas, Talbot abandoned his plan to wait at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> for reinforcements. The French ordnance officer Jean Bureau had built an artillery park of ditch and earth wall with up to 300 guns.</p><p>Talbot reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libourne">Libourne</a> on 16 July with only 500 men-at-arms and 800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>. On 17 July he defeated a small French detachment at a priory and, told that the French were retreating, pushed on; the dust was in fact raised by camp followers leaving. The English ran into the full French force, and Talbot ordered his men to keep fighting. French guns destroyed the advancing soldiers, and his reinforcements arrived piecemeal to the same fate. After over an hour a thousand Breton cavalry under Peter II, Duke of Brittany, struck the English right flank and the English broke. Talbot and his son were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The survivors took refuge in the castle of Castillon, which surrendered on 19 July. English authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> eroded, and the French retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> on 19 October. The battle is cited as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> and was the first major European battle won through the extensive use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a>. The English crown lost all its continental possessions except Calais, lost in 1558, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a>. Henry VI lost his mental capacity in late 1453, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> began. Historians differ over the site of the French camp: one hypothesis puts it north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a>, another in front of the town itself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Castillon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English rule ended after the <b>Battle of Castillon</b> in July 1453, where French artillery broke the English attack and the commander John Talbot, Earl of Shrewsbury, was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>All was lost after the French recovery; defeat at the <b>Battle of Castillon</b> in 1453 left only Calais, which fell in 1558.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Recovery came with Joan of Arc's relief of Orléans in 1429 and with Charles VII's reforms, which in 1445 created the compagnies d'ordonnance, the first permanent royal cavalry, and used artillery to win at Formigny in 1450 and <b>Castillon</b> in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
